  "raw": " replaced as second Earl of Stafford, in a by-election by James Huddleston. He continued to retain his share in the estates and the Abbey gardens, in which he ultimately died in 1821.\n\nEarly life\nBurke was born in Fifeshire, Scotland, in 1762, the son of Abraham Burke, 8th Earl of Stafford, by Mary, daughter of John Hamilton, 6th Earl of Elgin.  The elder Burke was born outside of Dunkirk and the younger Burke was born in Fifeshire. Although he was named Abraham, he was born into the clan Angus, therefore another Burke, seated at Glencairn, in Fifeshire, was known as Abraham Burke, 7th Earl of Stafford, later before the formation of Staffordshire.  The text in the minutes of Stafford Parish register dated 6 January 1763, records the mother of Abraham Burke was Jean, daughter of Abraham Gilbert, 8th Earl of Carrick.\n\nBur",
  "serialized": "replaced as second Earl of Stafford, in a by-election by James Huddleston. He continued to retain his share in the estates and the Abbey gardens, in which he ultimately died in 1821. Early life Burke was born in Fifeshire, Scotland, in 1762, the son of Abraham Burke, 8th Earl of Stafford, by Mary, daughter of John Hamilton, 6th Earl of Elgin. The elder Burke was born outside of Dunkirk and the younger Burke was born in Fifeshire. Although he was named Abraham, he was born into the clan Angus, therefore another Burke, seated at Glencairn, in Fifeshire, was known as Abraham Burke, 7th Earl of Stafford, later before the formation of Staffordshire. The text in the minutes of Stafford Parish register dated 6 January 1763, records the mother of Abraham Burke was Jean, daughter of Abraham Gilbert, 8th Earl of Carrick. Bur",
